1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Tanya Cushman Reviewer Reviewer's Name

2
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:16,000
The human voice.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,600
It's the instrument we all play.

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:21,000
It's the most powerful sound in the world, probably.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,500
It's the only one that can start

6
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:24,000
a war or say, I love you.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,500
And yet many people have

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:27,000
the experience that when they speak,

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,400
people don't listen to them.

10
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:29,480
Why is that?

11
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:31,480
How can we speak powerfully

12
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:33,480
to make change in the world?

13
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:34,880
What I'd like to suggest,

14
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:36,280
there are a number of habits

15
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:37,680
that we need to move away from.

16
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:39,580
I've assembled for your pleasure

17
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:41,480
here seven deadly sins of speaking.

18
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:43,880
I'm not pretending this is an exhaustive list,

19
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:46,880
but these seven, I think, are pretty large.

20
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,080
Habits that we can all fall into.

21
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:51,880
First, gossip.

22
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:54,680
Speaking ill of somebody who's not present.

23
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,680
Not a nice habit, and we know perfectly well

24
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,880
the person gossiping five minutes later

25
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:01,880
will be gossiping about us.

26
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:03,480
Second, judging.

27
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:05,680
We know people who are like this in conversation,

28
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:07,480
and it's very hard to listen to somebody

29
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:09,480
if you know that you're being judged

30
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:12,480
and found wanting at the same time.

31
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:14,480
Third, negativity.

32
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:15,680
You can fall into this.

33
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,280
My mother in the last years of her life

34
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:19,480
became very, very negative, and it's hard to listen.

35
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:20,980
I remember one day I said

36
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:22,480
to her, it's October the 1st today,

37
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:24,880
and she said, I know, isn't it dreadful?

38
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:26,880
Laughter

39
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:28,680
It's hard to listen when somebody's that angry.

40
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:31,880
It's hard to listen when somebody's that negative.

41
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:34,679
And another form of negativity, complaining.

42
00:01:34,679 --> 00:01:38,679
Well, this is the national art of the UK.

43
00:01:38,679 --> 00:01:39,679
It's our national sport.

44
00:01:39,679 --> 00:01:42,080
We complain about the weather, about sport, about politics,

45
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,679
about everything, but actually complaining is viral misery.

46
00:01:45,679 --> 00:01:49,679
It's not spreading sunshine and lightness in the world.

47
00:01:49,679 --> 00:01:51,080
Excuses.

48
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:52,280
We've all met this guy.

49
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:53,880
Maybe we've all been this guy.

50
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:56,679
Some people have a blame-thrower.

51
00:01:56,679 --> 00:01:57,880
They just pass it on to everybody else,

52
00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:59,679
and don't take responsibility for their actions.

53
00:01:59,679 --> 00:02:01,179
And again, hard to listen

54
00:02:01,179 --> 00:02:02,679
to somebody who's being like that.

55
00:02:02,679 --> 00:02:05,579
Penultimate, the sixth of

56
00:02:05,579 --> 00:02:08,479
the seven, embroidery, exaggeration.

57
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:10,680
It demeans our language, actually, sometimes.

58
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:14,079
For example, if I see something that really is awesome,

59
00:02:14,079 --> 00:02:15,480
what do I call it?

60
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:18,079
Laughter

61
00:02:18,079 --> 00:02:20,480
And then, of course, this exaggeration becomes lying,

62
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:21,480
out and out lying, and we

63
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:22,480
don't want to listen to people

64
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:24,480
we know are lying to us.

65
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,680
And finally, dogmatism.

66
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:31,280
The confusion of facts with opinions.

67
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:32,980
When those two things get

68
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:34,680
conflated, you're listening into the wind.

69
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:36,680
You know, somebody is bombarding you with their opinions

70
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:38,080
as if they were true.

71
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:40,680
It's difficult to listen to that.

72
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:43,880
So here they are, seven deadly sins of speaking.

73
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:46,480
These are things I think we need to avoid.

74
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:49,080
But is there a positive way to think about this?

75
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:50,480
Yes, there is.

76
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:52,580
I'd like to suggest that

77
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:54,680
there are four really powerful cornerstones,

78
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:57,480
foundations that we can stand on, if we want to,

79
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:00,180
if we want our speech to be

80
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,680
powerful and to make change in the world.

81
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:04,680
Fortunately, these things spell a word.

82
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:06,180
The word is hail, and it

83
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:07,680
has a great definition as well.

84
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:08,680
I'm not talking about the

85
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:09,680
stuff that falls from the sky

86
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:11,680
and hits you on the head.

87
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:13,680
I'm talking about this definition, too.

88
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:15,680
Greet or acclaim enthusiastically, which is, I think,

89
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:17,180
how our words will be received

90
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:18,680
if we stand on these four things.

91
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,680
So what do they stand for?

92
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:22,680
See if you can guess.

93
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:25,680
The H, honesty, of course.

94
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:26,680
Being true in what you say.

95
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:28,680
Being straight and clear.

96
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:30,680
The A is authenticity.

97
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,680
Just being yourself.

98
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:34,180
A friend of mine described it

99
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:35,680
as standing in your own truth,

100
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:37,680
which I think is a lovely way to put it.

101
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:39,680
The I is integrity.

102
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:40,680
Being your word.

103
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:42,680
Actually doing what you say

104
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:44,680
and being somebody people can trust.

105
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:47,680
And the L is love.

106
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:49,680
I don't mean romantic love, but

107
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:51,680
I do mean wishing people well.

108
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:52,680
For two reasons.

109
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:54,180
First of all, I think absolute

110
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:55,680
honesty may not be what we want.

111
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:57,680
I mean, my goodness, you look ugly this morning.

112
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:01,680
Perhaps that's not necessary.

113
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,680
Tempered with love, of course, honesty is a great thing.

114
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:07,680
But also, if you're really wishing somebody well,

115
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:10,680
it's very hard to judge them at the same time.

116
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,680
I'm not even sure you

117
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:14,680
can do those two things simultaneously.

118
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,680
So hail.

119
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:18,180
Also, now that's what you say,

120
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,680
and it's like the old song.

121
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:20,680
It is what you say.

122
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:22,680
It's also the way that you say it.

123
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:23,680
You have an amazing toolbox.

124
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:25,680
This instrument is incredible.

125
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,180
And yet this is a toolbox

126
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:28,680
that very few people have ever opened.

127
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:30,180
I'd like to have a little

128
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:31,680
rummage in there with you now.

129
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:33,180
Just pull a few tools out that you

130
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:34,680
might like to take away and play with,

131
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,680
which will increase the power of your speaking.

132
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:39,680
Register, for example.

133
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:41,680
Now, falsetto register may not be

134
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:43,680
very useful most of the time.

135
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,680
But there's a register in between.

136
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:47,180
I'm not going to get very technical about

137
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:48,680
this for any of you who are voice coaches.

138
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:50,680
You can locate your voice, however.

139
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,180
So if I talk up here in

140
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:53,680
my nose, you can hear the difference.

141
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:54,680
If I go down here in my throat,

142
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:56,680
which is where most of us

143
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,680
speak from most of the time,

144
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,680
but if you want weight, you need

145
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:02,680
to go down here to the chest.

146
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:04,680
You hear the difference?

147
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,680
We vote for politicians with lower voices.

148
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:08,680
It's true.

149
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:13,680
Because we associate depth with power and with authority.

150
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:15,680
That's register.

151
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:17,680
And we have timbre.

152
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:19,680
It's the way your voice feels.

153
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:21,680
Again, the research shows that we

154
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:23,680
prefer voices which are rich, smooth, warm,

155
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:25,680
like hot chocolate.

156
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:27,680
Well, if that's not you, that's

157
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:29,680
not the end of the world.

158
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:31,680
Because you can train.

159
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:32,680
Go get a voice coach.

160
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:34,180
And there are amazing things you

161
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:35,680
can do with breathing, with posture,

162
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:38,680
with exercises to improve the timbre of your voice.

163
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:41,680
Then prosody. I love prosody.

164
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:43,680
This is the sing-song, the meta-language that

165
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:45,680
we use in order to impart meaning.

166
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:47,680
It's route one for meaning in conversation.

167
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,680
People who speak all on one note

168
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:51,680
are really quite hard to listen to

169
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:53,680
if they don't have any prosody.

170
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:56,680
That's where the word monotonic

171
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:59,680
comes from, or monotonous, monotone.

172
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:02,680
Also, we have repetitive prosody now coming in,

173
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:05,680
where every sentence ends as if it were a question.

174
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:08,680
When it's actually not a question, it's a statement.

175
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:12,680
And if you repeat that one over and over,

176
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:14,180
it's actually restricting your

177
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:15,680
ability to communicate through prosody,

178
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:17,680
which I think is a shame.

179
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:20,680
So let's try and break that habit.

180
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:21,680
Pace.

181
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:22,680
I can get very, very excited

182
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:23,680
by saying something really, really good.

183
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:24,680
I can do it really, really quickly.

184
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:28,680
Or I can slow right down to emphasize.

185
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:30,680
And at the end of that,

186
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:32,680
of course, is our old friend silence.

187
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:36,680
There's nothing wrong with a bit

188
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:38,680
of silence in a talk, is there?

189
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:41,680
We don't have to fill it with ums and ahs.

190
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:43,680
It can be very powerful.

191
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:45,180
Of course, pitch often goes

192
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:46,680
along with pace to indicate arousal,

193
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:48,680
but you can do it just with pitch.

194
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:50,680
Where did you leave my keys?

195
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:52,680
Where did you leave my keys?

196
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:55,680
Slightly different meaning in those two deliveries.

197
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:57,680
And finally, volume.

198
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:01,680
I can get really excited by using volume.

199
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:03,680
Sorry about that if I startled anybody.

200
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:05,680
Or I can have you really

201
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:07,680
pay attention by getting very quiet.

202
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:10,680
Some people broadcast the whole time.

203
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:11,680
Try not to do that.

204
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:13,680
That's called sodcasting.

205
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:17,680
Imposing your sound on people

206
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:19,680
around you carelessly and inconsiderately.

207
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:20,680
Not nice.

208
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:21,680
Of course, where this all comes from,

209
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:23,180
most of all, is when you've

210
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:24,680
got something really important to do.

211
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:26,180
It might be standing on a stage

212
00:07:26,180 --> 00:07:27,680
like this and giving a talk to people.

213
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:31,180
It might be proposing marriage, asking for

214
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:34,680
a raise, a wedding speech, whatever it is.

215
00:07:34,680 --> 00:07:36,680
If it's really important, you owe it

216
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:38,680
to yourself to look at this toolbox

217
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:41,680
and the engine that it's going to work on.

218
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:44,680
And no engine works well without being warmed up.

219
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:46,680
Warm up your voice.

220
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:48,680
Actually, let me show you how to do that.

221
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:50,680
Would you all like to stand up for a moment?

222
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:54,680
I'm going to show you the six vocal warm-up exercises

223
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:57,680
that I do before every talk I ever do.

224
00:07:57,680 --> 00:07:59,180
Any time you're going to

225
00:07:59,180 --> 00:08:00,680
talk to anybody important, do these.

226
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:02,680
First, arms up.

227
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:05,680
Deep breath in and sigh out.

228
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:06,680
Like that.

229
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:08,680
One more time.

230
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:11,680
Very good.

231
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:13,680
Now we're going to warm up our lips.

232
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:15,680
And we're going to go bop,

233
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:17,680
bop, bop, bop, bop, bop, bop, bop.

234
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:18,680
Very good.

235
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:19,680
And now,

236
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:24,680
just like when you were a kid.

237
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:26,680
Now your lips should be coming alive.

238
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:29,680
We're going to do the tongue next with exaggerated

239
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:32,679
la, la, la, la, la, la, la, la, la.

240
00:08:32,679 --> 00:08:33,679
Beautiful.

241
00:08:33,679 --> 00:08:34,679
You're getting really good at this.

242
00:08:34,679 --> 00:08:35,679
And then roll an R.

243
00:08:37,679 --> 00:08:39,679
That's like champagne for the tongue.

244
00:08:39,679 --> 00:08:41,679
Finally, and if I can only do

245
00:08:41,679 --> 00:08:43,679
one, the pros call this the siren.

246
00:08:43,679 --> 00:08:44,680
It's really good.

247
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:46,680
It starts with we and goes to or.

248
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:47,680
The we is high, the or is low.

249
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:50,680
So you go, weeeeee.

250
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:51,680
Or.

251
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:52,680
Weeeeee.

252
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:53,680
Fantastic.

253
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:54,680
Give yourselves a round of applause.

254
00:08:54,680 --> 00:08:55,680
Take a seat.

255
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:56,680
Thank you.

256
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:58,680
Next time you speak, do those in advance.

257
00:08:58,680 --> 00:08:59,680
Now, let me just put this in context to close.

258
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:00,680
This is a serious point here.

259
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:01,680
This is where we are now, right?

260
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:02,180
We speak not very well into people

261
00:09:02,180 --> 00:09:02,680
who simply aren't listening in an environment that's

262
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:03,680
all about noise and bad acoustics.

263
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:04,180
I have talked about that

264
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:04,680
on this stage in different phases.

265
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:05,180
What would the world be like if we

266
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:05,680
were speaking powerfully to people who are not listening?

267
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:06,680
I mean, I think that's a very important thing.

268
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:07,680
I think that's a very important thing.

269
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:08,680
I think that's a very important thing.

270
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:09,680
I think that's a very important thing.

271
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:10,680
I think that's a very important thing.

272
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:11,680
I think that's a very important thing.

273
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:12,680
I think that's a very important thing.

274
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:14,670
In an environment that's all about noise

